[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: Authored full unit test suite for dag_engine_impl: DAG graph mutations/queries/validation (NodeNotFoundError, CycleError, topological tie-breaking, critical path), WaveScheduler wave selection/state transitions/failure policies, and DAGRunner success/retry/failure-policy/timeout/cancellation reporting, all within lifecycle phase scopes.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine.py: Implemented interface module: dataclass value types (NodeDefinition, NodeResult, DAGRunReport), exception hierarchy with message-based str/args, NodeState/FailurePolicy enums, and DAG/WaveScheduler/DAGRunner protocol declarations with operation stubs matching the low-level spec; package-qualified pretense import.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Implemented the concrete DAG engine: lock-protected adjacency graph (add/remove nodes and edges, definition and neighbor queries, validate with missing-dependency and DFS cycle detection, deterministic priority/alphabetical topological sort, roots/leaves, longest weighted critical path), wave scheduler with READY/PENDING synchronization, dependency-gated waves, terminal-state detection and failure policies (fail-fast cancellation, downstream skipping), and a concurrent DAGRunner with ThreadPoolExecutor waves, retries, per-node and overall timeouts, cancellation event, and consolidated DAGRunReport.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.mark_failed.continue_on_error] [viol:post.state_mutation_omitted] Facet: dependent non-terminal state retention Witness: policy=CONTINUE_ON_ERROR, failed='a', dependent='b', exp=PENDING, obs=SKIPPED
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_pretense.py: Implement DAG engine interface pretense state machine: faithful graph/scheduler/runner model state with 1:1 transitions, concrete argument domains, synthesized fake, fixed fake scenario suite covering all transitions and the seeding transition, and fake hypothesis fuzzing.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: Implement DAG engine implementation pseudo-state machine overriding all interface transitions with call_target pivots and SUT postcondition assertions, plus real implementation scenario tests driving the checked wrappers for full coverage and real hypothesis fuzzing over the assembled SUT.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Resolved feedback: WaveScheduler.mark_failed now applies policy-specific transitions only. FAIL_FAST still cancels remaining unfinished nodes and SKIP_DOWNSTREAM still marks transitive dependents SKIPPED, while the continue-on-error policy transitions only the failed node to FAILED and retains all dependent non-terminal states (blocked dependents remain PENDING, unblocked independent nodes stay eligible). Dispatch already stops when no ready wave remains, so retained dependents cannot spin the runner.
[AUDIT] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: QA_AUDIT
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: [slug:dag.dag.add_node.register_definition] [viol:pret_qa.overspecified_oracle] Facet: implementation suite action fixture Witness: action=<function _noop_action>, call='add_node(node_id, action, dependencies, priority, timeout_seconds, retries)'. The implementation pseudo-state machine's add_node override and the TestDagEngineImplReal scenarios pass a module-private duplicate zero-argument callable named _noop_action instead of the DAG model's canonical seeded action value, so every add_node dispatch from the real scenario tests and every fuzz-generated add_node rule is rejected by the model's argument contract with PretenseSpecificationError before the real DAG service is ever exercised; the specification declares action as any Callable[[], Any], so the implementation suite must rely on the model's single canonical action value rather than shadowing that name with a divergent local definition, and the same discrepancy recurs in test_graph_conformance, test_scheduler_conformance, test_runner_conformance and the DagEngineFuzzTest minimal counterexample.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: [slug:dag.dag.add_node.register_definition] [viol:cov.unreachable_under_spec] Facet: node re-registration edge repair Witness: missing_spans=[110] The stale-upstream purge in add_node only executes when a caller re-registers an existing node identifier, a scenario the low-level specification does not contract: node_id is documented as a unique identifier and add_node's contracted postconditions only require registering the definition and adding directed edges from the provided dependencies, with no replacement semantics for prior upstream edges. No conforming contract test can exercise this uncontracted replacement behavior, so the branch is unreachable under the specification closure and must be pruned or marked unreachable with # pragma: no cover (assumption: add_node is not re-invoked for an already registered node_id).
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: [slug:dag.dag.topological_sort.dependency_order] [viol:cov.unreachable_under_spec] Facet: dangling downstream neighbor guard Witness: missing_spans=[224] The guard that skips a downstream neighbor absent from the node registry cannot trigger under the specification closure: add_node and add_edge only create downstream edges pointing at registered nodes, and the contracted remove_node postcondition purges every incident edge, so every downstream neighbor of a registered node is itself registered. This is unreachable defensive code; prune it or mark it unreachable with # pragma: no cover (assumption: adjacency sets are only mutated through contracted add_node, add_edge, and remove_node calls).
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: [slug:dag.runner.run.timeout_limit] [viol:cov.unexercisable_defensive_guard] Facet: per-node timeout enforcement Witness: missing_spans=[494,496-500,502-505,509-511] The specification contracts DAGTimeoutError only for the run-level condition WHEN overall execution exceeds run's timeout_seconds (the exception is documented as raised when overall DAG execution exceeds the allocated timeout), and no contracted slug requires honoring a per-node timeout or converting it into a node failure. The helper-thread per-node timeout path, including its timeout raise and its re-raise of captured action exceptions, therefore has no contracted requirement to exercise and is uncontracted supplementary code beyond the specification closure. Prune this path or mark it unreachable with # pragma: no cover (assumption: DAGTimeoutError applies only to overall run elapsed-time overrun, not per-node timeouts).
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.runner.run.abort_on_cancel] [viol:cov.missing_contract_postcondition] Facet: cancellation during active execution Witness: node_states=[RUNNING,CANCELLED] contracted_error=[DAGCancelledError] The authored suite claims timeout and cancellation reporting but never exercises the contracted precondition that a cancellation signaled while execution is in progress marks non-terminal nodes CANCELLED and raises DAGCancelledError; the covered cancellation cases are only pre-run status queries and cancellation of unstarted nodes, so the during-execution cancellation contract remains unverified.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.mark_failed.skip_downstream] [viol:cov.missing_branch] Facet: convergent diamond transitive dependents Witness: graph=a->b,a->c,b->d,c->d states=[FAILED,SKIPPED] The SKIP_DOWNSTREAM postcondition requires every transitive dependent of the failed node to reach SKIPPED, but the suite only covers a linear dependency chain where each dependent is discovered along a single path; the conforming convergent-diamond case, where one transitive dependent is reachable through two distinct dependency paths and must still be transitioned exactly once, is unexercised.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.mark_succeeded.promote_dependents] [viol:cov.missing_branch] Facet: dependent already terminal before promotion Witness: dependent_state=SKIPPED upstream_policy=SKIP_DOWNSTREAM The promote-dependents postcondition only promotes dependents that are still PENDING; a dependent already driven to a terminal state because another upstream dependency failed under SKIP_DOWNSTREAM must remain terminal when a remaining upstream dependency succeeds, and that non-PENDING dependent path is never exercised by the authored suite.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.dag.critical_path.longest_path] [viol:cov.missing_boundary_endpoint] Facet: empty graph critical path Witness: dag=empty path=[] weight=0.0 The longest-path postcondition has no empty-graph boundary case: with zero nodes the contracted longest path is the empty sequence with cumulative weight zero, yet the suite only exercises non-empty chains and diamonds, so the empty-graph endpoint of the critical-path contract remains unexercised.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.dag.remove_node.purge_incident_edges] [viol:cov.missing_contract_postcondition] Facet: removed node scheduling state Witness: removed_node_state=absent After a node is removed from the graph, the scheduler-reported state set must enumerate exactly the nodes still present in the graph, but the authored suite only removes nodes before any scheduling state exists, so the removal of a previously reported node state is never exercised.
[BLAME] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/tests/dag_engine_impl_test.py: [slug:dag.scheduler.mark_failed.continue_on_error] [viol:cov.missing_branch] Facet: retained blocked dependent run termination Witness: failure_policy=CONTINUE_ON_ERROR dependent_state=PENDING Under CONTINUE_ON_ERROR a failed node's dependents retain non-terminal PENDING states while independent nodes stay eligible, so a run whose only remaining nodes are those retained dependents must still terminate and return a consolidated report; the authored suite exercises continue-on-error only with a succeeding independent sibling and never a run where retained dependents leave no schedulable wave.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/lib/dag_engine_impl.py: Resolved coverage feedback: (1) marked the uncontracted add_node re-registration upstream repair with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: add_node is not re-invoked for an already registered node_id)'; (2) pruned the unreachable dangling-downstream-neighbor guard from topological_sort since contracted add_node/add_edge only create registered dependents and remove_node purges incident edges; (3) pruned the uncontracted per-node helper-thread timeout machinery, invoking node actions directly and retaining only the contracted run-level timeout enforcement in _check_abort.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_pretense.py: Resolve pret_qa overspecified-oracle feedback: expose the DAG model's single canonical action value as public noop_action used by the model and both suites, and widen the add_node action argument domain to an open domain (Domain.any) biased toward the canonical value so any contract-valid Callable[[], Any] is accepted instead of only a private fixture.
[CHANGE] benchmark_4/parts/dag_engine/pretense/dag_engine_impl_pretense.py: Resolve pret_qa overspecified-oracle feedback: the implementation pseudo-state machine's add_node override and all TestDagEngineImplReal scenarios now dispatch the DAG model's single canonical noop_action value imported from the interface pretense module, removing the duplicate module-private callable so no add_node dispatch is rejected by the model's argument contract; real DAG service execution proceeds through the checked wrappers as intended.
